{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "id": "prior-auth-mri",
  "title": "MRI prior authorization turnaround",
  "turns": [
    {
      "jobId": "5aa52edc-7a48-416b-aba4-3af764880640",
      "question": "Does a cardiac MRI need prior authorization, and how many business days does the payer have to decide? What is the expedited turnaround?",
      "submittedAt": "2026-10-03T04:17:58.856980Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-10-03T04:18:03.321634Z",
      "status": "success",
      "report": {
        "markdown": "**Yes, a cardiac MRI requires prior authorization.**\n\n| Item | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Service category** | Advanced imaging (examples: MRI, CT, PET scans, **cardiac MRI**) |\n| **Prior authorization required?** | Yes |\n| **Standard decision turnaround** | 5 business days |\n| **Expedited decision turnaround** | 24 hours |\n| **Submit request at least** | 5 business days before the scan |\n\nThe policy notes that the decision clock starts when the payer receives a **complete** request. An expedited request is allowed when the ordering provider attests that the standard wait would seriously jeopardize the patient’s health or ability to regain function (e.g., a cardiac MRI for new symptoms with rising troponin) [1].\n\n## Sources\n\n- [1] Unstructured Retrieval evidence — prior_authorization_policy.pdf — evidence `hermes-receipt:01977598aee8caf79cfa9e7d31fb847547dd66c4310bb4151d7a19079b4e76f7`",
